<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Manado</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Menado</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Manado is the capital of North <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sulawesi">Sulawesi</a> province in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indonesia">Indonesia</a>, a port at the northern tip of Sulawesi. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch-east-india-company">Dutch East India Company</a> built a fort there in the seventeenth century, and the surrounding Minahasa region became largely Christian under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> rule. Its people were recruited in large numbers into the colonial army and administration, and Manado remains a commercial centre of eastern Indonesia.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/manado.jpg" alt="Manado" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Christian Gloor · CC BY 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Soekarno_bridge_sunset.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Manado lies on the Bay of Manado, surrounded by mountains, with a tropical rainforest climate and no real dry season. The name comes from the Sangir word manaro, meaning on the far coast or in the distance, and first referred to the farther of two islands seen from the mainland. When the settlement there moved to the mainland the name came with it, and the island became Manado Tua. A world map by the French cartographer Nicolas Desliens, which shows the island as Manarow, mentions it first.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Before Europeans arrived, the area was ruled by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultan">Sultan</a> of Ternate, who collected tribute and introduced <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a>. The Portuguese made the sultan their <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassal</a> and set up a trading post at Wenang. The Spanish took the Portuguese fortress at Amurang in the 1550s with native allies and built a fort at Manado, and one of the first Indo-Eurasian communities of the archipelago developed there in the 16th century. The first King of Manado, Muntu Untu, in 1630, was the son of a Spanish mestizo. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> later renounced its Minahasa possessions to the Portuguese for 350,000 ducats, and the Minahasa, allied with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a>, expelled the last Portuguese.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch-east-india-company">Dutch East India Company</a> built Fort Amsterdam in 1658, and missionaries spread <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a> there; the first church, the Oude Kerk, still stands as Gereja Sentrum. HMS Dover captured Manado in June 1810, and in 1830 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> exiled the Javanese prince <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diponegoro">Diponegoro</a> there. The Japanese captured it in January 1942, and Allied bombing badly damaged it. In 1958 the rebel movement <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/permesta">Permesta</a> moved its headquarters to Manado; the central government bombed the city in February and invaded in June. In 1962 it was declared capital of North Sulawesi.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Manado is the second largest city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sulawesi">Sulawesi</a> after Makassar. Most residents belong to the Minahasa people, and the main language is Manado Malay, a Malay-based creole. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a> is the majority religion, and the city holds a large <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christmas">Christmas</a> celebration each year. The Ban Hin Kiong Temple, founded in 1819, is the oldest temple in the city, and Bunaken National Park lies offshore.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Manado” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Dutch authority remained limited outside Java, and on Java itself it was challenged by Prince Diponegoro of Yogyakarta, whose rebellion of 1825–1830, the Java War, cost perhaps 200,000 Javanese lives before he was arrested during negotiations and exiled to <b>Manado</b> and later Makassar.</blockquote><a href="/empire/dutch-east-indies">Dutch East Indies · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Eastern varieties differ clearly from western ones: kita means “we, us” in the west but “I, me” in <b>Manado</b>, where “we” is torang, shortened from kita orang, “we people”, and “my name” is kita pe nama rather than the western namaku.</blockquote><a href="/language/malay">Malay · Change &amp; Descendants</a></li></ul></section>
